 ('p', 'If your real problem is that the image contains too much or the wrong area rather than being the wrong size, reach for the <a href="../tools/image-cropper.html">Image Cropper</a> instead, cropping changes what the image shows, while resizing changes how many pixels it uses to show it. Knowing which one you actually need is half the job.'),
 ('h2', 'Why quality drops when you resize the wrong way'),
 ('p', 'When software shrinks an image, it has to decide what colour each new, larger pixel should be, because several original pixels now map onto one. The method it uses for that decision is called the resampling algorithm, and it makes a visible difference. A crude method simply picks the nearest original pixel and throws the rest away, which produces jagged edges and a rough result. A good method averages the surrounding pixels intelligently, producing smooth, clean edges that look like a natural reduction of the original.'),
